CAT = "ukdscHygiene"; SEG = "HYG"
# Large wild game hygiene. FSA Wild Game Guidance (Oct 2024) applies in England, Wales and Northern Ireland;
# Deer Initiative England and Wales larder and carcass guides.
Q = [
("For deer supplied for human consumption, which organs form the \"green offal\" removed first?",
 "The stomach and intestines", ["The liver, spleen and kidneys", "The heart, lungs and windpipe", "The kidneys and the bladder"],
 "After killing, the stomach and intestines must be removed as soon as possible, and the animal bled if necessary. They may be disposed of safely at the kill site or at a larder if prompt removal is not compromised."),
("When should a deer be gralloched?",
 "As soon as possible after the shot", ["Only once it reaches the larder", "Within a few hours of the shot", "Only after the carcass has chilled"],
 "The gralloch should happen as soon as possible, preferably immediately. An intact carcass stays in good condition for only about an hour in cool conditions and as little as 15 minutes on a hot sunny day."),
("What is the first step of a gralloch?",
 "Check that the deer is dead", ["Tie off the oesophagus first", "Open the abdomen with a long cut", "Free the rectum from the pelvis"],
 "Always check for death first by approaching cautiously and checking for a blink reflex when the eye is touched. A remaining reflex means the deer must be dispatched."),
("What is bleeding by thoracic sticking?",
 "A stab above the breastbone to the chest", ["A long cut across the throat to the spine", "A deep cut through the flank into the gut", "A knife thrust at the back of the skull"],
 "Insert the knife immediately above the breastbone, pointing towards the heart so it penetrates the chest cavity, and work the blade to either side to sever the blood vessels so blood flows freely."),
("Why is the oesophagus tied off when the pluck is pulled back through the chest?",
 "To stop gut contents leaking out", ["To hold the heart in its position", "To make the neck easier to skin", "To weigh the carcass more easily"],
 "A knot or cable tie stops stomach content leaking. If a cable tie is used it must be removed once the gralloch is finished."),
("How should you open the abdomen at a gralloch?",
 "Groin to sternum, cut from inside out", ["Sternum to groin, cut through the hair", "Flank to flank across the belly", "Groin to sternum, cut inwards by stabbing"],
 "Make an incision from the groin to the base of the sternum without puncturing the gut. Cuts should be made from the inside out so as not to cut hair, and the knife point can be covered with a finger."),
("Which part of the gut is a major source of possible contamination at a gralloch?",
 "The rectum", ["The liver", "The heart", "The tongue"],
 "The rectum needs care. Options include tying a length off inside with a knot or cable tie and leaving full removal until the larder."),
("What is \"greening\" of a carcass?",
 "Contamination from gut left in", ["Moss and mould growing on the hide", "A fat colour change from the diet", "A change of colour from chilling"],
 "Over time even intact guts left in the carcass cause a form of contamination of the edible parts known as greening, which is why they are removed promptly."),
("A deer is shot on a hot, sunny day. About how long does an intact carcass stay in perfectly good condition?",
 "As little as 15 minutes", ["About 1 hour in the sun", "About 3 hours if hung in skin", "About 90 minutes with gut left in"],
 "The Deer Initiative says an intact carcass stays in good condition for an hour or so in cool conditions (10 degrees C or less) but as little as 15 minutes on a hot day exposed to the sun, and less if the gut is damaged."),
("Who must examine a deer carcase that is supplied to a game handling establishment?",
 "A trained person", ["A butcher", "The landowner", "A police wildlife crime officer"],
 "A trained person must examine the body and any removed viscera as soon as possible after killing, looking for characteristics that may indicate the meat presents a health risk."),
("When wild game is hunted with a view to placing it on the market, who must have trained person status?",
 "At least one active team member", ["Every member of the hunting team", "Only the landowner of the estate", "Only the game dealer who buys it"],
 "At least one active member of the hunting team must be a trained person with enough knowledge to carry out the initial examination of wild game."),
("Which topic must a trained person's training cover?",
 "Anatomy, physiology and behaviour", ["How to price and sell venison", "Rifle zeroing and ballistics", "Close season dates for every species"],
 "Training must also cover abnormal behaviour and pathological changes, hygiene rules and techniques for handling, transporting and eviscerating wild game, and the legislation on animal and public health and hygiene."),
("A trained person finds no abnormalities. What must they issue and attach to a large wild game carcase?",
 "A numbered declaration for the carcase", ["A copy of the shooting permission", "A weight and price ticket", "A note of the rifle and load used"],
 "The declaration is usually attached by a tag or label so it can be linked to the carcase. It states that no abnormal behaviour was observed before killing and there is no suspicion of environmental contamination."),
("A trained person declares no abnormalities. What need not accompany the body to the game handling establishment?",
 "The head and the viscera", ["The skin and the hooves", "The body of the animal itself", "The declaration and the tag"],
 "With a declaration and no abnormal characteristics, no abnormal behaviour and no suspected environmental contamination, the head and viscera do not need to accompany the body, except in species susceptible to Trichinosis such as wild boar."),
("A trained person suspects abnormal characteristics in a carcase. What must accompany the body?",
 "The head and viscera, but not the gut", ["The head and all viscera including the gut", "The head only, with no viscera", "The skin and the head, without any viscera"],
 "In that case the hunter must also inform the competent authority, the FSA Official Veterinarian, of the abnormalities that prevented a declaration. If a notifiable disease is suspected, contact APHA."),
("No trained person is available to examine the deer. What must go with the body to the game handling establishment?",
 "The head plus viscera excluding the gut", ["The head and all viscera including the gut", "The viscera only, without the head", "The head and the liver only"],
 "Without a trained person's examination, the head (not the antlers) and all viscera except the stomach and intestines must accompany the body, with adequate correlation maintained."),
("Whom should you contact if you suspect a deer has a notifiable disease?",
 "The Animal and Plant Health Agency", ["The Health and Safety Executive", "The local environmental health officer", "The Environment Agency"],
 "Notifiable diseases are those you are legally obliged to report to APHA, even if you only suspect an animal may be affected."),
("Large wild game meat should be chilled to a temperature throughout the meat of not more than:",
 "7 degrees C, without freezing", ["10 degrees C throughout the meat", "12 degrees C within 24 hours", "7 degrees C, then frozen hard"],
 "Chilling must begin within a reasonable period after killing and reach not more than 7 degrees C throughout the meat. The carcase should not be frozen."),
("Under the FSA guidance, what temperatures apply during transport?",
 "7 C large, 4 C small, 3 C offal", ["7 C for all game and offal alike", "10 C large, 7 C small, 4 C offal", "0 C large, 2 C small, 4 C offal"],
 "The cold chain must not be interrupted. Temperatures during transport are kept at 7 degrees C for large wild game and its meat (other than offal), 4 degrees C for small wild game and 3 degrees C for offal."),
("In the larder, at what temperature is a carcass ideally stored?",
 "Between 1 and 2 degrees C", ["Between 4 and 5 degrees C", "Between 6 and 7 degrees C", "Just below 0 degrees C"],
 "The Deer Initiative's larder design guide says ideally 1 to 2 degrees C, not frozen, and that once chilled a carcass should not be allowed to warm above 7 degrees C again."),
("How should carcasses be arranged during transport to a game handling establishment?",
 "Not piled, heaped or stacked", ["Stacked in two layers", "Closely packed so they stay cool together", "Wrapped tightly in plastic sheeting"],
 "Heaping carcasses on top of each other must be avoided as it prevents cooling and encourages cross-contamination."),
("Wild game kept only for your own private domestic consumption, or supplied to family and friends for theirs, is:",
 "Outside the food hygiene regulations", ["Always subject to a HACCP plan", "Needing local authority registration", "Needing an approved establishment"],
 "The food hygiene regulations do not apply to private domestic consumption, though the implied hygiene standards are still recommended. Family and friends must not supply it on."),
("You supply small quantities of in-skin deer carcasses directly to a local restaurant. What applies?",
 "General food law, with traceability", ["Full approval as an approved establishment", "No rules at all apply to you", "Only mandatory Trichinella testing"],
 "Under the primary producer exemption you must still supply safe food and keep records for traceability. Registration as a food business is not required but you should talk to your local authority."),
("What do the guides mean by \"local\" supply of deer carcasses to retailers?",
 "Same or neighbouring county, or 50 km", ["Anywhere in Great Britain at all", "Only within your own parish boundary", "Within a five mile radius of the larder"],
 "Local refers to the supplier's county, any neighbouring counties or a radius of 30 miles or 50 km, whichever is greater, and includes mail order and internet sales to the final consumer."),
("To supply carcasses to an approved game handling establishment, you must:",
 "Register with your local authority", ["Hold a venison dealer's police licence", "Pay an annual fee to the Deer Initiative", "Have a larder with a 20 square metre chiller"],
 "Suppliers to an approved game handling establishment need trained person status, registration as a food business with the local authority and a food safety management system based on HACCP principles."),
("What does traceability require along the supply chain?",
 "Records one step back, one step forward", ["Records held only by the final buyer", "A dated photograph of every deer shot", "A copy of the stalker's DSC1 certificate"],
 "Traceability needs the name and address of the supplier and customer, the date and the nature of the product, so food can be traced one step back and one step forward."),
("What should be done with a deer killed in a road traffic accident?",
 "It must not be supplied for human consumption", ["It may be sold to a dealer", "It may be supplied if gralloched within 10 minutes", "It may be sold if chilled to 7 degrees C"],
 "Wild game must only have been killed by hunting. Deer killed by any other means, such as a road traffic accident, must not be supplied for human consumption."),
("How should lead-shot game be prepared?",
 "Remove as much lead and meat around it", ["Cook it for longer to neutralise the lead", "Soak it in salt water overnight", "Freeze it first to loosen the shot"],
 "The FSA says to remove as much lead shot as possible and the surrounding meat. Minimising lead-shot game is especially important for children, pregnant women and women hoping to conceive."),
("What should be done with gralloch waste, as best practice?",
 "Bury it, incinerate it or double-bag it", ["Spread it thinly on the field margin", "Compost it in a heap on the holding", "Put it in a single bag in the household bin"],
 "Best practice is to incinerate or render it in an approved plant, bury it on the holding where the animal was shot, or double-bag it for landfill to prevent environmental contamination."),
("How should larder waste containers be marked?",
 "Not fit for human consumption", ["Hazardous waste, handle with care", "Keep chilled until collected", "Return to supplier after each use"],
 "Heads, legs and other waste go in impervious containers with well-fitting lids, marked \"Not fit for human consumption\"."),
("What is the recommended sterilising method for knives and saws in the larder?",
 "Water at 82 degrees C", ["Water at 60 degrees C", "Water at 72 degrees C", "A wipe with a damp cloth"],
 "The Deer Initiative lists water at 82 degrees C, ultraviolet light cabinets or sterilising wipes, which can also be used during use."),
("Which hand hygiene rule applies in a deer larder?",
 "Washing hands before each carcass", ["Washing hands only at the end of the day", "Using one pair of gloves for the whole session", "Rinsing hands under cold water only"],
 "Hands must be washed before working on each carcass or after touching anything potentially contaminated. Do not eat, drink or smoke in the larder."),
("A larder worker has a cut on the hand. What should they do?",
 "A blue waterproof plaster or glove", ["An ordinary fabric plaster on its own", "A waterproof plaster of any colour", "Clean kitchen paper held on with tape"],
 "Cover personal wounds with a clean waterproof, food-safe (blue) plaster or wear disposable gloves that are changed when soiled."),
("Which condition should keep a person out of the larder?",
 "Food poisoning symptoms", ["Wearing glasses or contact lenses", "A healed scar on one forearm", "Being tired after a long day"],
 "Do not enter the larder or dress carcasses if you, or people you have contact with, have food poisoning or septic lesions that cannot be covered or any other disease likely to be transmitted to the carcass."),
("How often should a chiller temperature be checked and recorded when in use?",
 "At least twice a day", ["Once a day at opening", "Once every two days", "Once a week on a set day"],
 "The Deer Initiative says chiller temperature should be checked and recorded at least twice a day when in use. Digital loggers mean checks need not be in person."),
("What should you do with a muddy deer carcass before taking it into the larder?",
 "Wash the hide, keeping cuts clean", ["Hang it in the larder as it is", "Hose it hard on full jet inside", "Skin it outdoors on the ground first"],
 "Dirty carcasses should not be brought directly into the larder. Wash the hide, taking care not to contaminate the sticking point, bullet wound and gralloch cuts."),
("Which supply route requires the hunter or a team member to be a trained person?",
 "Supply to an approved game handler", ["Venison eaten by your own family", "Direct supply to a local restaurant", "Deer shared with friends at home"],
 "Only supply to an approved game handling establishment makes trained person status a requirement. For direct supply to final consumers or local retailers it is encouraged but not required."),
("During a gralloch, what should you do when your gloves become soiled?",
 "Replace them or wash bare hands", ["Wipe them on a clean cloth and carry on", "Rinse them under the larder tap and reuse", "Put a second pair on over them"],
 "Gloves must be replaced, or bare hands washed or wiped, as they become soiled. Use clean equipment and keep it clean throughout."),
("Where is it best practice to complete carcass dressing?",
 "In a larder, minimum done in the field", ["On the ground where the deer fell", "At the roadside beside the vehicle", "Inside the back of the stalking vehicle"],
 "Best practice is the minimum in the field, completing dressing in the controlled environment of a larder. Because time is critical in preventing contamination, the gralloch often has to be done in the field."),
("What gap should a larder leave between hanging carcasses?",
 "At least 15 cm apart", ["No more than 2 cm apart", "About 5 cm apart, no more", "About 1 m apart or more"],
 "The larder design guide says allow at least 20 cm between wall and carcass and at least 15 cm between carcasses, with about 1 m of working space around a carcass."),
("What fall should a larder floor have towards the drains?",
 "About 1 in 60", ["About 1 in 6", "About 1 in 600", "About 1 in 20"],
 "Floors should have a fall of around 1 in 60 to suitably located drains, and drain inlets should have debris traps with mesh no bigger than 6 mm."),
("How should cuts through the skin be made when dressing a carcass?",
 "Outwards, with as few cuts as possible", ["Inwards, cutting through the hair", "Any number of cuts, made quickly", "Only after the carcass has been chilled"],
 "Minimise the number and extent of cuts and make them outwards rather than cutting inwards through the hair, which leaves cut hair everywhere."),
("What is a game larder under hygiene guidance?",
 "A facility for storing wild game", ["A shop selling venison in the market", "A vehicle used to drive deer around", "A room for cleaning and storing rifles"],
 "The FSA treats necessary carcass preparation such as bleeding and gralloching, and storage, at game larders as part of primary production. Larders must be protected against contamination from animals and pests."),
("How should a larder be protected against pests?",
 "Kept sealed, checked and recorded", ["Sealed, with poison bait left on the floor", "Sealed, and checked only if a pest is seen", "Sealed, with sticky traps hung over carcasses"],
 "Keep the larder sealed when not in use, make sure walls and doors are free from cracks and gaps, use insect electrocutor traps, and make and record regular checks."),
]
